## Complexity Analysis

| Algorithm | Time Complexity | Space Complexity |
|------------|----------------|------------------|
| Minimax | O(b^d) | O(d) |
| Alpha-Beta Pruning | O(b^d) Worst Case | O(d) |
| Alpha-Beta Pruning | O(b^(d/2)) Best Case | O(d) |
| Heuristic Alpha-Beta | O(b^k) | O(k) |
| MCTS | O(N × Simulation Length) | O(N) |

Where:

- `b` = Branching Factor
- `d` = Search Depth
- `k` = Depth Limit
- `N` = Number of Simulations

---

## Results

The implemented algorithms successfully identified optimal moves across multiple Tic-Tac-Toe board configurations.

### Observations

- Minimax always finds the optimal move.
- Alpha-Beta Pruning produces identical results while exploring fewer nodes.
- Heuristic Alpha-Beta improves efficiency using evaluation functions.
- MCTS provides strong move selection through repeated simulations.

---

## Technologies Used

- Python 3
- Object-Oriented Programming
- Artificial Intelligence Search Techniques

---

## Conclusion

This project demonstrates the implementation and comparison of four important AI search algorithms. The results validate their correctness and highlight the trade-offs between exhaustive search, pruning techniques, heuristic evaluation, and simulation-based decision-making.

